================================================================================
Regenerating the resize golden vectors
================================================================================

internal/plate/resize_golden_test.go pins resizePlanar against recorded output.
It is the only hermetic guard on the resampler: changing the weights or the
rounding leaves every other test green while detection confidence silently
drifts. The vectors were produced as follows.

Fixtures are self-describing — for source width sw, height sh and channel
count cn, the source buffer is interleaved, row-major, and filled with:

    src[i] = (i*37 + 11) % 251        for i in 0 .. sw*sh*cn-1

reshaped to (sh, sw) for cn=1 or (sh, sw, 3) for cn=3, dtype uint8.

The oracle is OpenCV:

    dst = cv2.resize(src, (dw, dh), interpolation=cv2.INTER_LINEAR)

flattened in the same interleaved row-major order. Verified against
opencv-python-headless 4.10.0.84 and 5.0.0.93, which produce byte-identical
output; every case matched elementwise with zero differing elements.

MUST be regenerated on x86-64. OpenCV ships architecture-specific HAL backends
on aarch64 — Carotene, and KleidiCV in wheels from 4.13.0.92 — that deviate
from OpenCV's own generic C++ path by up to 4/255. Running the same fixtures on
an arm64 wheel disagrees (one case differed on 3071 of 9800 elements), and
which shapes and channel counts deviate varies by wheel and by size. An arm64
result is an invalid oracle, not something to work around. On Apple Silicon,
use Docker with --platform linux/amd64.

The same x86-64 rule applies to regenerating the integration baseline above.

Note that this parity covers resampling only. Go's JPEG decoder differs from
libjpeg-turbo in chroma upsampling and IDCT, so JPEG input does not reproduce
the baseline; see the preprocessing invariants in AGENTS.md.
